the spookiest time of year is quickly  approaching and if you're looking for a  gory movie marathon you could do a lot  worse than the Friday the 13th series  Friday may not be the father of the  slasher genre but it's definitely the  crazy uncle and the unstoppable Jason  Voorhees the signature hockey mask is  now one of the most recognizable icons  in American pop culture for newcomers  and hardcore fans alike we're running  down every Friday the 13th movie ranked  from worst to best after sprinting past  the events of the original film the 2009  reboot moves on to an adult Jason with  an intelligent cunning characterization  that seemed like a pointless change if  not an outright betrayal of the original  series  Jason's are seemingly supernatural  abilities are neatly explained away  callbacks to the previous films for  ridiculously flat and the Jason captures  a girl and holds her prisoner as if it  ever bother with mere kidnapping the  film had a so-so performance at the box  office but earned dismal reviews and a  lengthy tug-of-war over rights issues  helped sync another attempt at a reboot  this film simply shouldn't exist even  the worst entry from the original series  is preferable on that note  just one in a long line of films that  were meant to end the series Jason goes  to hell the final Friday and does the  mythology built by the previous entries  reinventing Jason as a vengeful spirit  that can only be killed by members of  his own bloodline using a special magic  dagger after being killed by the FBI in  the beginning  Jason spends most of the movie as a  spirit possessing random people by  forcibly installing his heart into their  chest cavity's it's only notable moments  come at the very end when an appearance  by a familiar knife gloved hand sets up  a much better movie that wouldn't show  up for another decade a film that  definitely fails to live up to its title  1989's Friday the 13th part 8 Jason  Takes Manhattan benefits only from the  presence of Kane Hodder in a second turn  as the man behind the mask a ridiculous  amount of the movie takes place in the  confines of a cruise ship or Jason's  actual romp through the Big Apple it  takes up perhaps 20 minutes at the  movies end you don't  there is a maniac trying to kill us  welcome to New York  of course director Rob Hedden had  intended something very different as he  explained in a making-of book jason was  supposed to murder his way through all  sorts of iconic New York locations  before jumping off the Statue of Liberty  but the measly five million dollar  budget wouldn't allow for any of that  instead of that awesome sounding movie  we got one of the most visually flat  tedious entries in the entire series  every long-running horror franchise  eventually embarrasses itself by sending  its villain into space so it's only  fitting that the final proper  installments of the original Friday the  13th series would do just that after  being captured by the government  Jason is placed into cryogenic stasis  for hundreds of years  revived by some students he weeks his  special brand of havoc on a spaceship  receives an insane cyborg upgrade and as  once again seemingly dispatched for good  despite its silly premise mm and once  Jason X is surprisingly solid with a  ferocious performance by Hodder and his  final outing and at least a couple of  the series most truly bizarre and  inventive kills a late film sequence  featuring cyborg Jason in a holodeck  like recreation of Camp Crystal Lake  it's also a nice touch and the features  are remixed a version of what hot accord  is favorite kill of all time which just  so happens to be featured in the next  entry despite being little remembered  1988 Friday the 13th part 7 the new  blood is noteworthy for marking Kane  Hodder's debut as Jason and also for the  infamous sleeping bag scene  the film's protagonist is Tina Shepard a  telekinetic younger woman who  inadvertently revives Jason from his  watery grave at the bottom of Crystal  Lake while trying to bring her drowned  father back to life many fans felt that  the Jason vs. Carey premise was a bit of  a stretch but harder held the film  together and the even helped the film  set a record for stunt work in the  film's climactic battle  Tina uses her powers to set Jason on  fire a scene that was achieved by having  hotter actually be on fire for a  whopping 40 seconds as the cameras  rolled his dedication to the role showed  LA and his Hut performance helps make  the new blood a solid entry it wasn't  until 2003 that fans hopes on the  promise made in the last few frames of  Jason goes to hell were finally  fulfilled  fortunately the movie of fans nightmares  ended up being worth the years long wait  while it isn't actually part of the main  Friday the 13th franchise the crossover  Freddy vs. Jason earns its place in the  list by virtue of being so awesomely  over-the-top  a Nightmare on Elm streets Freddy  Krueger is bummed that his memory among  the living is dying out making it harder  for him to invade the dreams of children  he decides to free Jason Voorhees from  hell and turn him loose on his old Elm  Street stomping grounds in the hopes  that the fresh wave of gruesome murders  will rekindle the legend of Freddy but  once Jason starts killing it's pretty  tough to get him to stop  so Freddy maneuvers Jason into a  showdown before he can kill off all the  Freddy's potential victims the film  works far better than it has any right  to after spending an eternity in  development hell the franchisor styles  mesh well and there are a couple of  genuinely creepy moments amidst the  carnage the fifth film in the series  1985 s Friday the 13th a new beginning  is a near-perfect Friday the 13th film  that fans absolutely hate for one  totally understandable reason the ending  of the movie reveals that the Jason is  been killing his way through a teen  rehab facility wasn't ever actually  Jason at all but just some random guy  specifically a paramedic upset over the  death of his son early in the film okay  the Jason copycats weak motivation was  especially disappointing considering the  interesting psychological thriller angle  the film takes before the reveal and our  teenage Tommy Jarvis who has a child put  a brutal end to Jason's reign of terror  with the murderers own machete is among  the residents at the home for troubled  teens when the murders begin anew the  implication that Tommy may be  responsible for his new round of mayhem  is teased throughout and his ultimate  faceoff of the Jason copycat is truly  nerve jangling but for most fans for  fake out sending wasted all the goodwill  the movie engendered with it's crazy  kills and high body count it all makes a  new beginning a very good franchise  entry with one king-sized floor that  ultimately shouldn't keep her from being  enjoyed the third entry in the Friday  the 13th franchise had the misfortune of  being produced in 1982 smack in the  middle of the early 80s 3d revival but  if you can get past all the unnecessary  3d shots that only exist to throw  something at the audience part three is  a tight suspenseful shocker that  features some jaw-dropping kills and  sees Jason put on his iconic hockey mask  for the first time the film's plusses  nothing special  Jason kills a bunch of teenage friends  and their biker gang rivals producers  toyed  the idea of definitively ending the  franchise with this entry scripting a  conclusion in which Jason's head is  lopped off by protagonist Chris but  ultimately Jason's fate has left hanging  after a nightmarish false ending that  illustrates how fake out dream sequences  should be done at a scant 87 minutes  1981 so Friday the 13th Part 2 is short  dark and efficient when the carnage  starts it comes fast and furious the  movie wastes no time in ceremoniously  dispatching alice the sole survivor of  the first film within its first few  minutes this installment Jason is a mute  ferocious beast and the means by which  he's defeated confusing him by invoking  the memory of his dead mother would  become a reliable means of at least  slowing him down in future entries in  the series despite the departure of the  first film special effects wizard Tom  Savini part to offer - sequences  creative enough to invoke the wrath of  the MPAA  who insisted that a total of 48 seconds  be cut to avoid an X rating it's still a  brutal relentlessly paced entry and a  worthy sequel to one of the greatest  slasher films of all time the film that  launched a seemingly unstoppable  franchise is actually more of a murder  mystery that most of the slasher flicks  it inspired the third act to reveal that  the killer is former camp Kirk misses  for he's driven insane by the drowning  death of her weirdo son Jason was  absolutely jaw-dropping as was her  ultimate fate thanks to the film special  effects genius Tom Savini according to  director Sean Cunningham Savini obsessed  endlessly over how to accomplish mrs.  Voorhees death scene and he also  designed the young Jason's look for his  surprise appearance at the end Friday  the 13th was a film born of pure  money-making opportunism but savvy knees  groundbreaking make-up effects work  turned it into a horror classic of  course it also doesn't hurt that the  performance of veteran actress Betsy  Palmer as mrs. Voorhees it's super  creepy kill her mommy  the second best film when the franchise  has at all tons of campy kills skin  crawling scares and great performances  all around the film opens with a  delirious sequence in which Tommy Jarvis  literally digs up Jason's grave on a  stormy night impales the corpse with an  iron steak from the cemetery's fence and  angrily tosses a hockey mask and machete  into the grave  anybody who's ever seen Frankenstein can  guess what happens next there's a freak  lightning strike and a newly reanimated  Jason an explicitly supernatural being  for the first time in the series goes on  a killing spree  the opening sets a self-referential tone  that continues throughout the movie  successfully making the audience's laugh  between the jump scares and brutal  murders the film is simply a blast the  body count is absurdly high and stuntman  CJ graham is second only to Kane Hodder  in his menacing portrayal of Jason it's  almost the best Friday the 13th ever  almost as the name suggests the fourth  installment of the series was indeed  intended to be the final one and if it  had been the franchise may have had a  completely different legacy the film  begins with Jason escaping the morgue  and getting set to embark on another  rampage before finally meeting as much  in an unlikely adversary Jason's our  soon-to-be longtime nemesis Tommy Jarvis  makes his first appearance as a 12 year  old played by a younger Corey Feldman  Tommy ends up saving a sister's life in  the film's nerve-wracking climax one of  the most intense and graphic final  scenes in any mainstream horror film ask  any truly hardcore fan and they'll tell  you the final chapter is a  quintessential Friday the 13th film  unbelievably brutal and at times  unbearably tense it's not only the best  film and the franchise but one of the  greatest slasher films ever check out  one of our newest videos right here plus  even more looper videos about your  favorite stuff are coming soon subscribe  to our YouTube channel and hit the bell  so you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

